WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.158
Does a racing heart always mean fear?

00:00:03.158 --> 00:00:06.316
Running, anticipation and tension can raise it.

00:00:06.316 --> 00:00:09.474
Emotion research contains multiple theories.

00:00:09.474 --> 00:00:12.632
Body, context and interpretation offer clues.

00:00:12.632 --> 00:00:15.789
One expression cannot contain a whole experience.

00:00:15.789 --> 00:00:22.105
Notice the body’s signals.

00:00:22.105 --> 00:00:28.421
Consider what happened and what matters.

00:00:28.421 --> 00:00:34.737
Try a provisional name for the feeling.

00:00:34.737 --> 00:00:41.053
Weather is a metaphor for complex experience.

00:00:41.053 --> 00:00:44.211
One bodily response can fit several emotions.

00:00:44.211 --> 00:00:47.368
One emotion need not have one fixed expression.

00:00:47.368 --> 00:00:50.526
It cannot justify a diagnosis of anyone.

00:00:50.526 --> 00:00:56.842
You can feel both nervous and hopeful.

00:00:56.842 --> 00:01:03.158
Ask what preparation, rest or support you need.

00:01:03.158 --> 00:01:06.316
Significant physical symptoms need appropriate care.

00:01:06.316 --> 00:01:09.474
Feelings need not be sorted into good and bad.

00:01:09.474 --> 00:01:12.632
Describe more. Rush to labels less.

00:01:12.632 --> 00:01:15.789
Leave an open window for inner weather.
